// Reference QR symbols, pinned. Not written by hand, and not generated by the
// encoder they check.
//
// **Where these came from.** Each entry is the matrix another implementation
// produced for the same text, byte mode, error-correction level M, version
// range 1-10, mask left to the implementation: **qrcodegen 1.8.0** (PyPI, the
// Python edition of Project Nayuki's QR Code generator), run on
// **2026-09-22** as
// `QrCode.encode_segments([QrSegment.make_bytes(text)], Ecc.MEDIUM, 1, 10,
// -1, False)`. It was chosen because it hands over the module matrix, the
// version and the mask number rather than an image, and because it is a
// reference implementation in another language: a matrix that agrees with it
// module for module has agreed about the Reed-Solomon blocks, the interleave,
// the placement order, the mask penalty and both BCH codes at once.
//
// **Checked a second way.** Every one of these symbols, as drawn by
// `encode.ts`, was rendered to a bitmap and read back by **zxing-cpp 3.1.1**
// (PyPI, the C++ ZXing port) on **2026-09-22**; all fifteen decoded to their
// input text. Matching one encoder could mean sharing its mistake; being read
// by a decoder that was written by neither is the second source.
//
// **One implementation was tried and set aside, and it is worth saying why.**
// segno 1.6.6, read the same day, emits an extra `0x00` codeword between the
// terminator and the pad codewords in byte mode — visible by reading the
// codewords back out of its matrices. ISO/IEC 18004 §7.4.9 and §7.4.10 pad to
// the byte boundary and then go straight to `0xEC 0x11`, and the standard's
// own worked example (version 1-M, `01234567`, numeric) does exactly that, as
// do qrcodegen and this encoder. In byte mode the message always lands on a
// byte boundary after the four-bit terminator, so segno spends one pad
// codeword on nothing. The symbols still read; they are just not the same
// symbols, which is why they cannot be the vector.
//
// **This is output, not source.** `docs/OPEN-QUESTIONS.md` A3 limits borrowed
// code; a test vector is a fact about what a correct symbol looks like, and
// `encode.ts` was written from ISO/IEC 18004. Pinning the matrices is what
// makes the check survive none of these tools being installed.
//
// The ladder covers every version this encoder supports, 1 to 10: each block
// plan in `tables.ts`, both character-count widths (8 bits to version 9, 16
// from version 10) and the version-information block that appears at version
// 7. `mask` is recorded because a wrong penalty score picks a different mask
// and every module then differs — a vector that did not pin it would hide it.
//
// Written 2026-09-22.

export interface QrVector {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly text: string;
  readonly version: number;
  readonly mask: number;
  readonly size: number;
  /** One string per row, `1` for a dark module. */
  readonly rows: readonly string[];
}

